Well, let's see.  I first played Rogue back in '88 or so.  I had a
version of Hack around 1990.  I've played nethack on and off every
couple of years for the last decade.  Finally, a few months ago I
decided to make a sincere attempt to ascend.

After a long string of unsuccessful Wizards, I ran through several
promising Valkyries and Barbarians but could never get the finish
right.  One even learned the hard way about drawbridges and wands of
force bolt.

Finally, I switched back to Wizards-- weak at the beginning but the
most satisfying to play in the end-- and lost one Wizard on my first
visit to the Astral Plane to Death himself.  The biggest flaw with that
game?  Until then, in all those years, I had never realized that a bag
could be (a)pplied instead of dropped and looted, and incredibly poor
luck had resulted in the loss of that Wizard's BoH on the PoE (a
monster standing atop the teleporter while the bag sat on the ground,
one too many keypresses after killing it) and then the back-up sack on
the PoF (fire vortex scooping him up and dancing around only to
regurgitate him... you guessed it... on the teleporter.)  As such, that
Wiz was sorely underequipped on the Astral Plane and was set upon by
Death extremely fast.

After that experience, I did some googling to see if that had happened
to anyone else, and I quickly put 2 and 2 together... people were
(a)pplying bags rather than dropping and looting them.

A bunch of other lessons learned, in terms of preparation for the
ascension run, too.

This character was played on and off over the last few weeks.  Early
boots of speed helps greatly.  Minetown had a co-aligned altar, which
was highly beneficial, and resulted in being gifted Magicbane,
something I'd discovered was a virtual must-have for Wizards.  With MB,
I sacrificed spell reliability for AC in the early game with dwarven
mithril, and had an okay time, except for almost losing it to a bones
file that had a Minotaur just below Minetown along with the body of one
of my previous incarnations.

A new co-aligned altar appeared on level 9 and another spate of
sacrifices provided a few spellbooks and then Firebrand.  This gift,
which set the broadsword skill unlocked, gave my mage a very
high-powered weapon.

Sokoban provided the much needed BoH.  For once I couldn't
reflection-kill Medusa and took her out blindfolded.  (Perseus's statue
had nothing but a sack!)  So when I got to the Castle, lots of Sleep
spells and Magic Missiles eventually cleared out the mess and the
wishing wand bought SDSM to go under the CoMR (I like this better for
mages than GDSM + AoR because it frees up the neck for the Eye.)  Added
a helm of brilliance too.  A captain in the castle provided a
pre-enchanted blessed +4 silver sabre, for all my demon and undead
needs.

Scouring Gehennom and rampant polypiling provided the other items I
needed, except for two.  One was a spellbook of FoD: I couldn't get
crowned for the life of me.  Eventually, right before taking on Rodney,
I would spend a wish on that.  The other was an amulet of magic
breathing, which would require the last wish before I exited the PoE.

I tackled the named demons with relative ease... Juiblex never even
heard me coming this time, Asmodeus was dispatched after I stood on the
upstair, and Orcus was tricky with some monster summoning and a wand of
death but I picked him off after a brief chase.  Vlad provided as much
of a challenge as usual-- ie. very little.

At this point I climbed back to my main spellbook store on level 8 and
refreshed my many expired spells, did a massive alchemizing of
accumulated PoH and PoEH and boosted my hit points into the high 200s.
Genocided the usual suspects, and then some more for good measure
thanks to spare scrolls.

Magicbane and FoD means never having to suffer Rodney very long, and in
fact he came back just the mandatory once: a far cry from past games of
getting bogged down and cursed into oblivion.  The PoA was somewhat
challenging, but not like the time I lost a Valk there with multiple
Rodneys, hundreds of monsters, no ring of Conflict and no way to find
the portal or get back the AoY.  The PoW was annoying because the
located trap ended up being missed for quite a long time because it was
under an object.

The Astral Plane was handled much better this time, I blinded to find
the less Deathy riders and try them.  Charging scrolls and the WoD
helped augment my own manna-draining FoD for the priests and keeping
Pestilence on the floor.  But he was guarding Thoth's altar, not
Anhur's.  So I gulped, fought my way to Famine's lair, and was starting
to get worn down a little before I broke through and-- to my relief, I
would not have to face Death again: my altar was there, the amulet was
plunked down as Famine came charging up towards me again, and it was
all over but for the screen-capturing.


